{"id": "language-p00944", "blocks": [{"id": "language-p00944", "text": "15. Resolved Conflicts and Superseded Drafts", "style": "HEADING_1", "sourceParagraph": 944}, {"id": "language-p00945", "text": "The following earlier claims remain historical evidence but are not part of the current canonical standard.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 945}, {"id": "language-p00946", "text": "EZ-SUP-0001 — harmonic_families", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 946}, {"id": "language-p00947", "text": "Earlier claim: Families are permanently tied to fixed home chords/keys such as LIF=C major and MOV=G major.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 947}, {"id": "language-p00948", "text": "Canonical replacement: EZ Core 1.0 uses transposable relative interval signatures; C examples are references only.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 948}, {"id": "language-p00949", "text": "Source nodes: 254", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 949}, {"id": "language-p00950", "text": "Replacement nodes: 258, 271", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 950}, {"id": "language-p00951", "text": "EZ-SUP-0002 — trit_encoding", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 951}, {"id": "language-p00952", "text": "Earlier claim: Trits 0, 1, and 2 are directly realized as a chord's root, third, and fifth.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 952}, {"id": "language-p00953", "text": "Canonical replacement: Trits are relative movements: stay, rise two semitones, fall two semitones.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 953}, {"id": "language-p00954", "text": "Source nodes: 254", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 954}, {"id": "language-p00955", "text": "Replacement nodes: 258, 271", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 955}, {"id": "language-p00956", "text": "EZ-SUP-0003 — trit_encoding", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 956}, {"id": "language-p00957", "text": "Earlier claim: One trit step is an unspecified scale step.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 957}, {"id": "language-p00958", "text": "Canonical replacement: One DBA step is exactly two semitones.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 958}, {"id": "language-p00959", "text": "Source nodes: 258, 260", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 959}, {"id": "language-p00960", "text": "Replacement nodes: 271", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 960}, {"id": "language-p00961", "text": "EZ-SUP-0004 — lexicon", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 961}, {"id": "language-p00962", "text": "Earlier claim: Life roots use unrelated forms such as mi, yu, nima, nimu, mir, nir, and num-lif.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 962}, {"id": "language-p00963", "text": "Canonical replacement: Life compact roots are generated by the universal three-trit compact-root algorithm.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 963}, {"id": "language-p00964", "text": "Source nodes: 254", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 964}, {"id": "language-p00965", "text": "Replacement nodes: 280, 284, 287", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 965}, {"id": "language-p00966", "text": "EZ-SUP-0005 — lexicon", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 966}, {"id": "language-p00967", "text": "Earlier claim: Motion roots use unrelated forms such as gan, gin, kim, gam, gat, gir, and gun.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 967}, {"id": "language-p00968", "text": "Canonical replacement: Motion roots use the same local address root as every family, qualified as gi·root.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 968}, {"id": "language-p00969", "text": "Source nodes: 254", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 969}, {"id": "language-p00970", "text": "Replacement nodes: 302", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 970}, {"id": "language-p00971", "text": "EZ-SUP-0006 — transliteration", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 971}, {"id": "language-p00972", "text": "Earlier claim: The compact root by itself is sufficient as the ordinary written root.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 972}, {"id": "language-p00973", "text": "Canonical replacement: Canonical human transliteration includes the family onset and middle dot.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 973}, {"id": "language-p00974", "text": "Source nodes: 280", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 974}, {"id": "language-p00975", "text": "Replacement nodes: 302", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 975}, {"id": "language-p00976", "text": "Note: The local root remains valid in harmonic performance and as an explicit local-root field.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 976}, {"id": "language-p00977", "text": "EZ-SUP-0007 — runtime_state", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 977}, {"id": "language-p00978", "text": "Earlier claim: State 0 descends one scale step before stopping.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 978}, {"id": "language-p00979", "text": "Canonical replacement: State 0 descends exactly one semitone and stops sharply.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 979}, {"id": "language-p00980", "text": "Source nodes: 254", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 980}, {"id": "language-p00981", "text": "Replacement nodes: 271", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 981}, {"id": "language-p00982", "text": "EZ-SUP-0008 — examples", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 982}, {"id": "language-p00983", "text": "Earlier claim: Tak mi, wi dak. Yamir wi: da-1 is a canonical Era Zero apology.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 983}, {"id": "language-p00984", "text": "Canonical replacement: Its roots come from the superseded arbitrary vocabulary and cannot be a canonical regression phrase under the fixed compact-root algorithm.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 984}, {"id": "language-p00985", "text": "Source nodes: 254", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 985}, {"id": "language-p00986", "text": "Replacement nodes: 271, 280, 302", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 986}, {"id": "language-p00987", "text": "EZ-SUP-0009 — examples", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 987}, {"id": "language-p00988", "text": "Earlier claim: Yu da. Mi yu wu. Nim num. and Mi-am dak tan... are direct Era Zero forms.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 988}, {"id": "language-p00989", "text": "Canonical replacement: Later source identifies these as later-era/placeholder approximations, not the final engineered Era Zero encoding.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 989}, {"id": "language-p00990", "text": "Source nodes: 227, 232, 236, 240", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 990}, {"id": "language-p00991", "text": "Replacement nodes: 242, 244, 254, 271", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 991}, {"id": "language-p00992", "text": "EZ-SUP-0010 — pronunciation", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 992}, {"id": "language-p00993", "text": "Earlier claim: Early placeholder syllables can be assigned independently to semantic roots.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 993}, {"id": "language-p00994", "text": "Canonical replacement: Formal carriers and compact roots are generated algorithmically from family and address.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 994}, {"id": "language-p00995", "text": "Source nodes: 149, 154, 164, 168", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 995}, {"id": "language-p00996", "text": "Replacement nodes: 271, 280, 302", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 996}], "outline": [{"id": "language-p00944", "title": "15. Resolved Conflicts and Superseded Drafts", "level": 1}, {"id": "language-p00946", "title": "EZ-SUP-0001 — harmonic_families", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00951", "title": "EZ-SUP-0002 — trit_encoding", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00956", "title": "EZ-SUP-0003 — trit_encoding", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00961", "title": "EZ-SUP-0004 — lexicon", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00966", "title": "EZ-SUP-0005 — lexicon", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00971", "title": "EZ-SUP-0006 — transliteration", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00977", "title": "EZ-SUP-0007 — runtime_state", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00982", "title": "EZ-SUP-0008 — examples", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00987", "title": "EZ-SUP-0009 — examples", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00992", "title": "EZ-SUP-0010 — pronunciation", "level": 2}]}